th Hemorrhagic and Infectious Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.6308411214953271, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal cardiopulmonary exam and stable asthma reduce likelihood of B3 (systemic sepsis) but don't eliminate localized infection (B1) as the primary source of i…"], "evidence_against": ["The unilateral discharge pattern slightly reduces support for B1's mucosal spread theory but doesn't eliminate it as localized vascular pathology might still a…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "ENT Process with Foreign Body Obstruction in Upper Respiratory Tract", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.252, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Acute hemodynamic features suggest rapid progression incompatible with B2's typical gradual presentation, reducing its plausibility."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Acute Systemic Infectious Process Complicating Mucosal Disease", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.10514018691588785, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Acute hemodynamic features suggest rapid progression incompatible with B2's typical gradual presentation, reducing its plausibility."], "evidence_against": ["Normal cardiopulmonary exam and stable asthma reduce likelihood of B3 (systemic sepsis) but don't eliminate localized infection (B1) as the primary source of i…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Chronic ENT Structural Lesion with Superimposed Acute Changes", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Hematologic/Immunologic Process with ENT and Hemodynamic Manifestations", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis with Complications (Etiology: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3823279524214103, "posterior": 0.3823279524214103, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Nasal Mucosal Vasculitis / Kiesselbach Plexus Erosion with Secondary Infection", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.19116397621070516, "posterior": 0.19116397621070516, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 0.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.0, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Pediatric Mucosal Trauma with Secondary Infectious Hemorrhage", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.057, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Septic Shock due to Community-Acquired Bacterial Sinusitis (Mostly Streptococcus pneumoniae/H influenzae)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.07359813084112149, "posterior": 0.07359813084112149, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Meningitic Complication of Sinusitis (Neisseria meningitidis / Hemophilus influenzae type b)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.021028037383177572, "posterior": 0.021028037383177572, "danger": 0.95, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 1.0, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Disseminated Coagulopathy Complicating Infection (e.g., Staph aureus toxemia with purpura fulminans)", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.010514018691588786, "posterior": 0.010514018691588786, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.15, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "pathophysiology"}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the unilateral purulent/bloody discharge with concurrent hypotension/tachycardia suggest a vascular source (Kiesselbach plexus) with secondary systemic instability rather than isolated sinus pat…", "summary": "Unilateral vascular discharge supports Kiesselbach plexus involvement (B1) but hypotension/tachycardia raises concern for systemic complications (B3) rather than isolated sinus inflammation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the treatment resistance pattern with decongestants argue for mechanical foreign body obstruction (B2) over mucosal vaso-inflammation (B1)?", "summary": "Treatment resistance implies B2 remains plausible, but unilateral involvement alone is insufficient to re-evaluate B1 (vascular lesion with local infection) over B3 (systemic infection)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of high fever or oral mucosal membranes argue against generalized vasculitis as a pathophysiology for systemic infection (B3)?", "summary": "Lack of fever/acute systemic signs weakens B3 (systemic vasculitis/sepsis) as the primary mechanism, though hemodynamic instability remains partially consistent with it."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the normal cardiopulmonary exam findings and well-controlled asthma contradict systemic sepsis (B3) but support localized vascular infection (B1)?", "summary": "Normal cardiopulmonary exam and stable asthma reduce likelihood of B3 (systemic sepsis) but don't eliminate localized infection (B1) as the primary source of instability."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypotension/tachycardia without fever contradict foreign body (B2)'s typical insidious progression with local symptoms over days?", "summary": "Acute hemodynamic features suggest rapid progression incompatible with B2's typical gradual presentation, reducing its plausibility."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would evidence of bilateral nasal involvement more strongly support B1's mucosal pathology than B3's septic focus?", "summary": "The unilateral discharge pattern slightly reduces support for B1's mucosal spread theory but doesn't eliminate it as localized vascular pathology might still apply."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.517], ["B2", 0.31], ["B3", 0.155]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.631], ["B1.1", 0.382], ["B2", 0.252]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hypotension with compensatory tachycardia suggests septic or hypovolemic shock requiring immediate fluid resuscitation while differentiating between B1 (localized vascular infection with systemic spread risk) and B3.1 (septic shock).", "required_actions": ["Establish IV access and initiate fluid bolus for hypotension", "Collect blood cultures prior to administering antibiotics", "Monitor central perfusion markers (capillary refill, skin perfusion)"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B1: 63.1%) remains below 0.75 confirmation threshold. Dangerous alternative B1.2 (vasculitis/erosion, danger 0.9, posterior 19.1%) and B3.1 (septic shock, danger 0.85, posterior 7.4%) are unresolved. Available discriminators (nasal endoscopy, imaging, lab tests) could refine diagnosis."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3823279524214103, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Bleeding and infected vessel of Kiesselbach plexus"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Foreign body obstruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Septal perforation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Sinusitis with bacterial superinfection"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Abnormal inflammatory response', 'Mild fever', 'Abnormality of the nasal septum'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.517}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis with Complications (Etiology: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae)", "posterior": 0.382, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1.2", "B3.1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=29%, source=dxs]\n\nENT Process with Foreign Body Obstruction in Upper Respiratory Tract vs Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis with Complications (Etiology: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae):\n  Favours first:  abscess formation, atelectasis, bluish skin discoloration, cavities, choking\n  Favours second: cough, ear pressure or fullness, facial pain or pressure, fatigue, headache\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.53):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > History and Physical\n  Children show slight differences in the clinical signs of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Besides the 10-day duration, key symptoms, and double worsening, children are more prone to have fevers. Initi\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.61):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > Introduction\n  Acute rhinosinusitis caused by viral upper respiratory infections and noninfectious conditions should be distinguished from acute bacterial rhinosinusitis based on illness pattern and duration of illn\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > Introduction\n  Acute rhinosinusitis caused by viral upper respiratory infections and noninfectious conditions should be distinguished from acute bacterial rhinosinusitis based on illness pattern and duration of illn\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  K. pneumoniae subspecies rhinoscleromatis is the causative agent of rhinoscleroma, a granulomatous mucosal upper respiratory infection that progresses slowly (over months or years) and causes necrosis\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms after initial decongestant treatment for acute sinusitis → bacterial superinfection of upper respiratory tract → Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis with Complications (Etiology: Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate signs of bacterial superinfection (fever >38.3°C, purulent discharge, prostration), which would upgrade to complicated bacterial sinusitis\n  hypotension (90/48 mmHg) and tachycardia (124 bpm) → bacteremia from unresolved sinusitis → Septic Shock due to Community-Acquired Bacterial Sinusitis (Mostly Streptococcus pneumoniae/H influenzae)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Assess for shock parameters (lactate >2 mmol/L, capillary refill >2 seconds) to confirm progression from sinusitis to septicemia\n  low-grade fever (37.5°C) with worsening nasal pain → osteomyelitis/meningitis progression → Meningitic Complication of Sinusitis (Neisseria meningitidis / Hemophilus influenzae type b)\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Monitor for new-onset nuchal rigidity, altered mental status, or Cushing's triad as sequelae of meningitic progression\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
